      <p class="byline">Lithium Knowledge · Systems · Last reviewed 2026-09-14</p>
      <h1>SOC vs SOH: The Two Numbers a Battery Management System Reports</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A battery management system reports two numbers that sound similar but answer different questions: how
        full the battery is, and how healthy it is. Confusing them is one of the most common mistakes in reading
        a battery's condition.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> <strong>SOC (state of charge)</strong> is the fuel gauge — the percentage of usable capacity
        available right now. <strong>SOH (state of health)</strong> is the aging report — how much of the battery's <em>original</em>
        capacity it still holds. They are independent: a battery can read 100% SOC while its SOH has fallen to 80%,
        because it still charges "full" — but that full is a smaller tank than it was when new.
      </div>

      <h2>What SOC measures</h2>
      <p>
        State of charge is the momentary energy level: 100% means fully charged, 0% means the usable capacity is
        exhausted. It is what a phone or EV dashboard shows as the battery bar. The <a href="/lithium/what-is-bms/">BMS</a> estimates it from cell
        voltage, current integration (coulomb counting) and temperature, and it resets to 0–100% on every cycle.
      </p>

      <h2>What SOH measures</h2>
      <p>
        State of health is the battery's remaining capacity relative to when it was new. A battery with 80% SOH has
        lost 20% of its original capacity to irreversible aging — mostly electrode degradation and electrolyte
        breakdown. SOH only moves in one direction (down) and does not reset.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the two decouple</h2>
      <p>
        The decoupling is the key insight. A degraded battery still charges to its own (now smaller) 100%, so SOC
        reads full while SOH has fallen. That is why "100% charged" says nothing about whether the battery is
        healthy — a 100% SOC / 60% SOH pack is full, but only holds a little over half its original energy. The
        usable energy is the <em>product</em>: SOC × SOH × original capacity.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this matters in practice</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>EV range</strong> — a car at 100% SOC with 80% SOH has lost ~20% of its original range, even though the gauge says full.</li>
        <li><strong>Second-life / resale</strong> — SOH is the number that determines a used battery's value, not SOC.</li>
        <li><strong>Warranty</strong> — many battery warranties are written against SOH (e.g. "70% SOH after 8 years"), not SOC.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What do people get wrong about SOC and SOH?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"100% charged means the battery is healthy."</strong> No — it means full. Health is SOH, a separate number.</li>
        <li><strong>"SOC and SOH are the same thing."</strong> They answer different questions: how full vs how degraded.</li>
        <li><strong>"SOH is measured directly."</strong> It is estimated — from capacity tests, <a href="/technology/internal-resistance/">internal resistance</a> and voltage behaviour, which is why different tools can report slightly different SOH.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>SOC</strong> = how full (fuel gauge); <strong>SOH</strong> = how degraded (aging report).</li>
          <li>A battery can be <strong>100% SOC but 80% SOH</strong> — full, but a smaller tank.</li>
          <li>Usable energy = <strong>SOC × SOH × original capacity</strong>.</li>
          <li>SOH is the number that matters for <strong>range loss, resale value and warranty</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <h2>The two numbers side by side</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>SOC</th><th>SOH</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Question</strong></td><td>How full is it now?</td><td>How much of its original self remains?</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Scale</strong></td><td>0–100% of current capacity</td><td>0–100% of original capacity</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Estimated by</strong></td><td>Voltage, coulomb counting</td><td>Capacity test, resistance trend</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Moves</strong></td><td>Every charge and discharge</td><td>Slowly, downward, with age</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>


            <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>SOC is the fuel gauge, SOH is the health check — a full tank in a worn battery is still a worn battery.</p>

      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>Where I stand:</strong> The SOC-vs-SOH distinction is the single most useful concept for judging a used
          battery — because "100% charged" is a statement about the last hour, while SOH is a statement about the
          battery's whole life.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why:</strong> Sellers quote SOC because it is flattering; buyers should ask for SOH because it is the
          truth. A used EV advertised as "fully charged" tells you almost nothing about what you are buying — the
          SOH tells you how much of the original battery you are actually getting. Learn to ask for SOH, and the
          ambiguity disappears.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          <em>This is my analysis, not a verified fact or purchasing guarantee.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the difference between SOC and SOH?</h3>
      <p>SOC (state of charge) is how full the battery is right now — a fuel gauge. SOH (state of health) is how much of its original capacity it still holds — an aging report. A battery can be 100% SOC but only 80% SOH, meaning it charges full but that full is a smaller tank.</p>
      <h3>Why does a battery show 100% SOC but 80% SOH?</h3>
      <p>Because the two measure different things. SOH reflects irreversible capacity loss from aging; SOC reflects the current charge relative to that reduced capacity. A degraded battery still charges to its (now smaller) full, so SOC reads 100% while SOH has fallen.</p>
<h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Does the battery report SOC and SOH separately, and can the buyer read both before purchase or use?</li>
  <li>Is the SOH figure derived from a full calibration cycle or an estimate that drifts?</li>
  <li>What SOH threshold does the warranty or the application consider end-of-life?</li>
  <li>Does the BMS expose the two numbers, or are they locked behind proprietary tools?</li>
</ul>

